# SQL Migrations

Run these files **in numeric order** against the `payroll_db` database.
Each file is run **exactly once**. Do not re-run old files.

| File | Purpose | Status |
|------|---------|--------|
| 001_initial_schema_and_seed.sql | Initial 20-table schema + seed data | ✅ Applied |
| 002_overtime_approval_workflow.sql | Overtime approval columns: status, time_in/out, requested_at, approved_by/at | ✅ Applied |
| 003_audit_log.sql | Audit trail table written centrally by respond() for every mutating request | ✅ Applied |
| 004_employee_contact_fields.sql | Employee self-service contact fields: mobile_number, email, address, emergency contacts | ✅ Applied |
| 005_payroll_approval_workflow.sql | Segregation of duties: payroll_runs status (Submitted/Approved/Returned/Released), submitter/approver/return/release columns + remarks | ✅ Applied |
| 006_employee_bank_fields.sql | Employee payment details for the bank file export: bank_name, bank_account_name, bank_account_number | Applied |

## Payroll approval workflow (005)

`payroll_runs.status` moves through:

```
Submitted --approve--> Approved --release--> Released
Submitted --return---> Returned  --resubmit-> Submitted
Returned  --approve--> Approved
```

* `Submitted` — the Payroll Officer (maker) processed the period.
* `Approved` / `Released` — locked: payslips, allowances, deductions, loan
  payments and cash disbursements are refused with HTTP 409 and a formal
  adjustment is required.
* `Returned` — the Approver sent it back with remarks; the run is editable
  again and the maker can resubmit it.

`payroll_db_schema.sql` (structure only) and `payroll_db_recovery.sql`
(structure + data, including the seeded `payroll` / `approver` / `admin`
accounts) are regenerated from the live database after every migration.

The three staff logins can be (re)created idempotently with:

```
C:\xampp\php\php.exe tests\seed_staff_accounts.php
```

| Account | Role | Password |
|---------|------|----------|
| payroll | Payroll Officer (maker) | test123 (never overwritten if it already exists) |
| approver | Approver (checker) | test123 (never overwritten if it already exists) |
| admin | Administrator (technical only) | unchanged |

## Employee bank details + payment exports (006)

`employees` gained three nullable columns:

| Column | Width | Purpose |
|--------|-------|---------|
| `bank_name` | VARCHAR(100) | the receiving bank |
| `bank_account_name` | VARCHAR(120) | account holder exactly as the bank knows it |
| `bank_account_number` | VARCHAR(40) | the account the bank file credits |

* They are edited on the Employees screen (Payment Details) and are
  MAKER-writable only: `employees.php` already requires `MAKER_ROLES` for
  POST/PUT, so the Approver can read them (needed to review the bank file) but
  cannot change them. A blank field is stored as NULL.
* They are never returned by the employee portal: every `me_*` endpoint selects
  its columns explicitly, so adding the columns cannot leak them.

`api/bank_file.php` is the single export endpoint:

```
GET api/bank_file.php?run_id=<id>&format=bank|register|remittance
```

| Format | File name | Contents |
|--------|-----------|----------|
| `bank` | `bank-file_<start>_<end>.csv` | one credit line per payslip + a TOTAL row |
| `register` | `payroll-register_<start>_<end>.csv` | every payslip: basic, overtime, gross, SSS/PhilHealth/Pag-IBIG employee shares, withholding tax, other deductions, totals + a TOTAL row |
| `remittance` | `govt-remittance_<start>_<end>.csv` | SSS / PhilHealth / Pag-IBIG employee AND employer amounts per employee + a GRAND TOTAL row |

Guard and refusals:

* `Payroll Officer` and `Approver` only (200). `Employee` and
  `Administrator` -> 403.
* Run missing -> 404; `format` not one of the three -> 422.
* Run status `Returned` -> 409 for every format: its figures are not final
  until the maker resubmits.
* `format=bank` -> 422 and no file at all when ANY included employee is
  missing `bank_name` or `bank_account_number`; the message names every
  affected employee (employee number + name).
* The CSV is `text/csv` with LF line endings, fields containing a comma, quote
  or newline are quoted, and a cell starting with `=`, `+`, `-` or `@` is
  prefixed with a single quote so it can never run as a spreadsheet formula.
* Exports are GETs and the central `respond()` hook only audits non-GET
  requests, so `bank_file.php` writes its own `audit_log` row for every attempt
  (successes and refusals alike). Account numbers are never stored in it.

`tests/bank_file_test.php` covers the three formats, the missing-bank-details
refusal, the Returned guard, the role guards, the CSV escaping and the audit
rows, and restores every fixture it creates.
